---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SeaBreak from '../../components/SeaBreak.astro';
import { celestial, scenes } from '../../config/images';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CTABand from '../../components/CTABand.astro';
const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Explore Astrology', href: '/explore/' },
  { label: 'Birth Chart Basics', href: '/explore/birth-chart-basics/' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Birth Chart Basics. How a natal chart works"
  description="A birth chart is a map of the sky at your moment of birth. Learn what planets, signs, houses, and aspects contribute, and why astrologers read them together rather than in isolation."
  path="/explore/birth-chart-basics/"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={crumbs} />
  <section class="hero hero--split">
    <div class="container">
      <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Start here</p>
      <h1>Birth chart basics</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        A birth chart, or natal chart, is a map of the sky calculated for the date, time, and place
        you were born. The map is made of many parts. Astrology begins by learning those parts and
        becomes much more interesting when you start seeing how they work together.
      </p>
      </div>
      <img
        class="hero__img"
        src={scenes.birthChartBasics.src}
        srcset={scenes.birthChartBasics.srcset}
        sizes="100vw"
        alt={scenes.birthChartBasics.alt}
        width={scenes.birthChartBasics.width}
        height={scenes.birthChartBasics.height}
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>What a chart actually is</h2>
      <p>
        Imagine standing where you were born at the moment you were born and mapping the sky all
        around you, including the half below the horizon. The positions of the planets, the zodiac,
        the horizon, and the meridian can all be calculated astronomically. Astrology begins when
        we interpret the relationships among those positions and ask what they may describe about
        a person's tendencies, experiences, and timing.
      </p>
      <p>
        I think that distinction matters. The planetary positions are astronomical data. What an
        astrologer makes of them is interpretation.
      </p>
      <h2>Four useful ways into the chart</h2>
      <p>For a beginner, four categories make the wheel much easier to understand:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong><a href="/explore/planets/">Planets</a></strong> describe different functions or
          drives within the chart. The same planet belongs to everyone, but it will not operate the
          same way in every chart.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><a href="/explore/signs/">Signs</a></strong> describe styles of expression. They
          modify how a planet tends to operate; they are not personalities by themselves.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><a href="/explore/houses/">Houses</a></strong> describe areas of experience. They
          help us ask where a planet or pattern may be most visible in a person's life.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><a href="/explore/aspects/">Aspects</a></strong> describe angular relationships
          among planets. They show us that the chart is not a collection of separate definitions:
          its parts are constantly modifying one another.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        You can learn each category separately, and that is exactly what the rest of this section
        will help you do. But no single planet, sign, house, or aspect tells the whole story. The
        work of interpretation is in the combination.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <SeaBreak image={celestial.galaxyTidepool} />

  <section class="section section--sea">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>Why your birth time matters</h2>
      <p>
        The rising sign is the zodiac sign on the eastern horizon at birth, and it changes
        throughout the day. The birth time also determines the chart's angles and house structure.
        That means two people born on the same date in the same city can share many planetary
        placements while having very different chart orientations. This is why astrologers ask for
        the most accurate birth time available. If you do not know yours,
        <a href="/birth-time/">there are still useful ways to work with what is known</a>.
      </p>
      <h2>Why a placement is never the whole person</h2>
      <p>
        Online astrology often makes one placement sound self-contained: "Mars in Aries means
        this" or "Moon in Pisces means that." Those descriptions can teach vocabulary, but they
        are only starting points. Mars may be in Aries, but it is also in a house, making aspects,
        ruling other parts of the chart, and operating inside one particular life.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is the part of astrology I find endlessly fascinating. The ingredients repeat across
        charts. The combinations do not. A chart becomes individual through relationship and
        context.
      </p>
      <h2>What a chart is not</h2>
      <p>
        A chart is not a verdict, a personality prison, or a set of instructions. It offers a way
        to look at patterns, tensions, capacities, and timing from another perspective. How those
        possibilities are lived depends on the person, the circumstances, and the choices made
        along the way.
      </p>
      <h2>Where to go next</h2>
      <p>
        Use the <a href="/tools/explore-your-chart/">interactive chart explorer</a> to see how the
        categories relate, or continue with <a href="/explore/the-big-three/">the Sun, Moon, and
        rising sign</a> before moving through planets, signs, houses, and aspects.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABand
    heading="The parts are general. The synthesis is personal."
    body="A natal reading is where we can look at how these pieces interact in your chart, how you recognize them in your life, and what is active now."
    buttonLabel="About the Natal Reading"
    buttonHref="/readings/natal/"
  />
</BaseLayout>
